The file system does not have directory structures and file names, and also
lacks metadata such as date/time, permissions, etc (these things can be
added by the data in forks, though).

It does have transactions (which can be nested), and each file contains
forks each of which is identified by a 32-bit integer, and the forks then
contain the byte streams. The byte streams can also contain links to other
files (or to itself).

The forks numbered 0x0000 to 0xFFFF will have standardized meanings, while
the other fork numbers have no standardized meanings and their meanings
will be specific to that file. Forks numbered 0xFFFFFF00 to 0xFFFFFFFF are
for internal use by the file system implementation and are not normally
accessible by application programs.

A link takes up eight bytes, but its value is not read like ordinary bytes
(when read by a program it appears as a capability value; see the "cpu"
file). The link references another file (or the same file), and can
optionally be specified as a "copy-on-write" link; if so then whenever the
referenced file changes (regardless of where the reference to that file
came from; not necessarily this link), it will make a copy and then the
link will point to the original unchanged file.


=== Standard forks ===

Some possibilities of standard forks (this specification might be changed
in future) might be:

0x0000 = Primary data in the standardized file format.

0x0001 = Byte stream data, for compatibility with programs for other
operating systems or VM codes, such as DOS or uxn programs.

0x0002 = Version control data (links to previous versions, etc).

0x0003 = Security information. Access to this fork is usually restricted.

0x0004 = Executable code.

0x0005 = Message schema. This would be needed to start a executable program
properly, so that it knows what schemas are possible for the initial
message, which should contain at least one capability since otherwise it
would not be able to do any I/O and would be terminated immediately unless
a debugger is attached.

0x0006 = Symbol table and debug information for executable files.

